-\abstract{Quantum tree-search frameworks require reversible bounding predicates, whose implementation costs can offset reductions in oracle queries. We study the linear-programming relaxation of the 0/1 knapsack problem under a fixed efficiency-ordered prefix branching rule. A classically certified finite bank of rational affine bounds preserves every integer-threshold pruning decision without reproducing the fractional bound itself. We derive coverage certificates, minimum-cardinality constructions on a density grid and over nonnegative rational multipliers, and a sufficient construction that avoids capacity-reachability dynamic programming. A counterexample family shows that a linear number of multipliers can still be necessary. The reversible construction uses constant-coefficient arithmetic, explicit register bounds, and compute--copy--uncompute cleanup. Exact arithmetic checks on 700 original instances and their 700 reduced cores cover 5,204,456 reachable states with zero mismatches in 20,817,824 bank-floor comparisons. Separate small-circuit tests report zero gate mismatches in 1,120 Fourier-arithmetic basis columns and 2,216 Boolean gate columns; these tests do not constitute large-instance circuit simulation. On 105 fixed-depth instances, derived logical resources relative to a root-aware fractional-scan reconstruction have median bank-to-control ratios of 0.4981 for controlled-NOT gates, 0.4908 for active qubits, and 1.0720 for depth. The results establish a scoped predicate-preservation construction and a gate-count--depth tradeoff, without establishing hardware advantage or an end-to-end quantum speedup.}
